--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
D. WHAT THIS RUN ESTABLISHES, AND THE TWO THINGS IT BROKE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT IT ESTABLISHES. The instrument runs on the real device and every refusal passes there: the
panic guard is disarmed (1 download_mode parameter reads 0), the cores are writable AND offer both
values, window A is the fix installed, and the write is proved by read-back on the device itself.
The trap's restore held: the device was left on `interactive`, read back after the run.

WHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH. The second cause's price in degrees. The verdict is CONTAMINATED: the
warming did not come back with the state, so this run says nothing about the governor.

AND THE SHAPE OF THAT CONTAMINATION IS THE INTERESTING PART -- it is not the drift the control was
built for. The three windows read, on the hottest zone: A 40.9, B 47.7, C 48.0. That is a monotone
rise across the whole run, not a bump in B that survives into C. The busy figures say the load did
not change (A 0.81, B 0.54, C 0.82 -- and B is the PINNED window with the LOWEST busy time, which is
the instrument's own documented reason for not treating busy as a gate). So the reading is consistent
with the intervention heating the SoC and 45 s of the fix's state not being enough to shed 7 C of
thermal mass -- which the control window CANNOT separate from a phone that is warming on its own.
The design's stated blind spot ("a drift that peaks in window B and vanishes in C is
indistinguishable from this effect") is not what happened here; what happened is a rise that does not
decay inside the window at all, and that is a second blind spot the design did not name.

DEFECT 1 (the instrument's message, and a second defect behind it). "... N more; the whole table is in
the archive (it is one line per zone)" -- the message names no PATH, and the directory it means has a
random suffix ($TMP/zl1-governor-temp-ab.XXXXXX). Worse, the same shape is in run_window ("its output
is in the archive"). The reading that follows is what the first version of this log got WRONG, and it
was settled by looking on the device rather than by reading the script:

    $ ls -d /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.*
    /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.HZcZCl        <- EMPTY: leaked by the --status run
    /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.MlHTie        <- this run: deltas, deltas.note, win.A, win.B, win.C
    /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.sh

The script DOES contain `trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT` -- at line 90, before the refusals. And at line 184
it contains `trap 'do_restore' EXIT`. In a POSIX shell a second EXIT trap REPLACES the first, so the
cleanup never runs at all: every invocation leaks its scratch directory, including the read-only
--status run (hence the empty one above). So the archive was never deleted -- the defect is that
(i) nothing is cleaned up, silently, and (ii) the message points at it without naming it, which for an
operator is the same thing. This is the "two traps, one EXIT, the second silently cancels the first"
shape, and it is invisible from the passing side: every assertion about the restore still holds.

DEFECT 2 (the design). A control window that assumes the intervention's effect decays faster than the
window is too weak for a cause whose effect is THERMAL MASS. The fix is not a longer settle chosen in
advance (that is the same guess one size up): the run has to WAIT for the reading to come back to
within a stated margin of A, with a bound, and then say how long it took -- and a return that does
not happen inside the bound is itself a measurement ("this intervention's warming does not decay
within N s"), not a verdict about the governor.

Both are fixable offline, and the harness has to fail on each before the next device run.

